Apply order 0026 against mariopartyrd/partyboard 98ac07fd1e8f7ab938bbb6fe75789fad99419010 (CC0-1.0; ADR 0009 section 5).
memory: HuMem returns zeroed blocks, on MPT_PC
Applied in order by runtimes/gc_wii/mp4/CMakeLists.txt with `git apply`.

ADR 0008, "Stale heap bytes: game heaps return zeroed blocks on PC". HuMem returned a block holding
whatever its earlier occupants left: on a 64-bit host often half of an 8-byte pointer to a function,
so the bytes depended on the module layout and differed between Windows and Linux (the layout
probe's full-party run split on such a word in a particle record at frame 82,000). HuMemMemoryAlloc2,
behind HuMemMemoryAlloc and HuMemMemoryAllocNum (and so HuMemDirectMalloc, its Num form and process
heaps), now clears the whole user area, to the end of the block, after the split and the header
write. The header layout and the constant caller address are unchanged. Zero-on-alloc, never on
free, so a use-after-free reads what it read before. Unconditional on PC.

diff --git a/src/game/memory.c b/src/game/memory.c
index ff17123..d575fd5 100644
--- a/src/game/memory.c
+++ b/src/game/memory.c
@@ -1,6 +1,10 @@
 #include "game/memory.h"
 #include "dolphin/os.h"
 
+#ifdef MPT_PC
+#include <string.h>
+#endif
+
 #ifdef SKIP_HU_ALLOC
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #endif
@@ -81,6 +85,11 @@ static void *HuMemMemoryAlloc2(void *heap_ptr, size_t size, uintptr_t num, uintp
             block->magic = 165;
             block->num = num;
             block->retaddr = retaddr;
+#ifdef MPT_PC
+            /* ADR 0008, "Stale heap bytes": a block's user area is zero when it is returned, whatever
+             * earlier allocations left there (on a 64-bit host often half of an 8-byte pointer). */
+            memset(BLOCK_GET_DATA(block), 0, (size_t)block->size - (size_t)(BLOCK_GET_DATA(block) - (char *)block));
+#endif
             return BLOCK_GET_DATA(block);
         }
         block = block->next;
